# Byte-pinned evidence assets.
#
# `board/board-manifest.json` records sha256 hashes of the EXACT bytes of the
# board assets, and the offline board tests verify those pins. This machine has
# `core.autocrlf=true` and the repository had no attributes file, so the same
# commit checked out with different bytes in different working trees: the
# canonical checkout held LF while a fresh worktree checkout held CRLF, making
# `command-board.json` 3,158 bytes larger and every byte pin fail. A Linux CI
# runner, which converts nothing, would produce a third result again.
#
# That is why the offline board suite passed for whoever ran it in the canonical
# directory and reported CONFLICT for anyone reviewing it elsewhere. Marking
# these assets as binary stops normalization touching them, so one commit means
# one set of bytes on every platform and in every checkout.
board/*.json -text
board/snapshots/** -text
